---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { href } from '../config'

const sections = [
  ['install', 'Install'], ['signin', 'Sign in to GitHub'], ['vault', 'Create a vault'], ['secret', 'Add a secret and use it'], ['apps', 'Use it in your apps'], ['node', 'Node.js and TypeScript'], ['vite', 'Vite'], ['flutter', 'Flutter'], ['servers', 'Servers and CI'],
]
---
<Base title="Docs" current="Docs">
  <div class="docs">
    <aside class="side">
      <div><h4>GETTING STARTED</h4>{sections.map(([id, t], i) => <a href={`#${id}`} aria-current={i === 0 ? 'true' : undefined}>{t}</a>)}</div>
      <div><h4>REFERENCE</h4><a href={href('/security/')}>Security model</a><a href={href('/changelog/')}>Changelog</a><a href={href('/download/')}>Download</a></div>
    </aside>
    <article class="article">
      <span class="crumb">Docs / Getting started</span>
      <h1 class="h1" style="font-size:2.75rem">Get started</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:640px">Install Gitvault, sign in with GitHub and store your first secret. It takes about two minutes, and the desktop app and the CLI use the same vault.</p>

      <h2 id="install"><i>01</i>Install</h2>
      <p>Download the installer for Windows or Ubuntu from the <a class="green" href={href('/download/')}>download page</a>. Gitvault needs git on your PATH.</p>

      <h2 id="signin"><i>02</i>Sign in to GitHub</h2>
      <p>Pick whichever fits. The GitHub CLI route needs no setup if you already use gh.</p>
      <div>
        <div class="opt"><b>GitHub CLI</b><p>Run gh auth login once, then choose "Continue with GitHub CLI" in the app.</p></div>
        <div class="opt"><b>Personal access token</b><p>Create a token with the repo scope and paste it into the app or pipe it to the CLI.</p></div>
        <div class="opt"><b>Device flow</b><p>Sign in from your browser. Needs an OAuth app with Device Flow enabled.</p></div>
      </div>

      <h2 id="vault"><i>03</i>Create a vault</h2>
      <p>A vault is a private GitHub repository holding only encrypted files. Choose a master password and save the recovery key shown once.</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">$</span> gitvault auth login --from-gh
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault vault create --name Personal
<span class="c"># prompts for a master password and prints your recovery key</span></pre>
      <div class="callout"><b>Keep your recovery key safe</b><p>It is the only way back in if you forget your master password. Gitvault cannot recover it for you.</p></div>

      <h2 id="secret"><i>04</i>Add a secret and use it</h2>
      <p>Add secrets in the app, or from the terminal. The value comes from stdin or a hidden prompt, never from an argument.</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">$</span> echo -n "sk_live_…" | gitvault secret add STRIPE_KEY \
    --environment production --value-stdin
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault secret list
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault run --environment production -- ./deploy.sh
<span class="c"># secrets injected as environment variables, never printed</span>
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault sync now</pre>

      <h2 id="apps"><i>05</i>Use it in your apps</h2>
      <p>Anything can run under the CLI: <code>gitvault run</code> injects an environment as variables and starts your command. Node.js, Vite and Flutter projects can also load secrets for you, with no .env file in the project.</p>

      <h3 id="node" class="sub">Node.js and TypeScript</h3>
      <p>The library is written in TypeScript, ships its own types, and works with both <code>import</code> and <code>require</code> on Node 18 or newer. It is published on the public npm registry as <code class="green">@adhil28/gitvault</code>, so no token or <code>.npmrc</code> is needed:</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">$</span> npm install @adhil28/gitvault</pre>
      <p>Load one environment before anything else runs:</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">import '@adhil28/gitvault/config'</span> <span class="c">// uses GITVAULT_ENV, then NODE_ENV</span></pre>
      <p>Or call it yourself. It is synchronous, returns only the names it set (never values), and keeps variables that are already set unless you pass <code>override</code>:</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">import</span> {'{'} config, GitVaultError {'}'} <span class="g">from</span> '@adhil28/gitvault'

<span class="g">try</span> {'{'}
  <span class="g">const</span> {'{'} environment, loaded {'}'} = config('production')
{'}'} <span class="g">catch</span> (e) {'{'}
  <span class="c">// e.code: E_LOCKED, E_PERMISSION_DENIED, E_NOT_AUTHENTICATED, E_NOT_INSTALLED ...</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      <p>The library asks the installed <code>gitvault</code> CLI, so the CLI must be on PATH (or set <code>GITVAULT_BIN</code>). Secrets are never logged or written to disk.</p>

      <h3 id="vite" class="sub">Vite</h3>
      <p>For Vite projects, add the plugin and drop the <code>.env</code> files. It loads one environment before Vite reads its own env, using the same <code>gitvault</code> CLI as the Node.js library, so it needs Node 18 or newer and Vite 5 or newer.</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="c">// vite.config.ts</span>
<span class="g">import</span> {'{'} defineConfig {'}'} <span class="g">from</span> 'vite'
<span class="g">import</span> {'{'} gitvault {'}'} <span class="g">from</span> '@adhil28/gitvault/vite'

<span class="g">export default</span> defineConfig({'{'}
  plugins: [gitvault()],
{'}'})</pre>
      <p>The environment is the one you pass (<code>gitvault({'{'} environment: 'staging' {'}'})</code>), then <code>GITVAULT_ENV</code>, then the Vite mode. <code>vite</code> uses <code>development</code>, <code>vite build</code> uses <code>production</code>, and <code>--mode staging</code> uses <code>staging</code>.</p>
      <p>Vite only hands variables that start with <code>VITE_</code> to your browser code, as <code>import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL</code>. Every other secret stays on the server side of the build. Name only values that are safe to publish with <code>VITE_</code>, because anything behind that prefix ends up in the built app.</p>
      <p>Variables already set in your shell win over the vault, and the vault wins over <code>.env</code> files. The plugin logs only how many variables it loaded, never names or values. If the CLI is missing or the vault is locked, the dev server stops with a clear error.</p>
      <p>The plugin runs after <code>vite.config.ts</code> has been read, so the config file itself cannot see vault values. If it needs one (a proxy target, say), load the vault first and skip the plugin. Pick the environment from the mode:</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">import</span> {'{'} defineConfig {'}'} <span class="g">from</span> 'vite'
<span class="g">import</span> {'{'} config {'}'} <span class="g">from</span> '@adhil28/gitvault'

<span class="g">export default</span> defineConfig(({'{'} mode {'}'}) =&gt; {'{'}
  config(mode === 'production' ? 'frontend-prod' : 'frontend-dev')
  <span class="g">return</span> {'{'}
    server: {'{'} proxy: {'{'} '/api': process.env.VITE_API_URL {'}'} {'}'},
  {'}'}
{'}'})</pre>

      <h3 id="flutter" class="sub">Flutter</h3>
      <p>Flutter apps run on phones and in browsers, where the <code>gitvault</code> CLI cannot run, so the values are added when you run or build. Put <code class="green">gitvault_flutter</code> in front of your Flutter command. It loads one environment and passes it to Flutter as <code>--dart-define</code> values. Add the package to <code>pubspec.yaml</code> from the repository (it is not on pub.dev yet):</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="c"># pubspec.yaml</span>
dev_dependencies:
  gitvault_flutter:
    git:
      url: https://github.com/adhil72/gitvault.git
      path: packages/flutter</pre>
      <p>The environment follows the build. A debug run gets <code>development</code>, a release build gets <code>production</code>, and a name you give wins over both:</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">$</span> dart run gitvault_flutter -- flutter run                <span class="c"># development</span>
<span class="g">$</span> dart run gitvault_flutter -- flutter build apk          <span class="c"># production</span>
<span class="g">$</span> dart run gitvault_flutter staging -- flutter build ios  <span class="c"># your choice</span></pre>
      <p>Read the values in Dart as constants:</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">const</span> apiUrl = String.fromEnvironment(<span class="g">'FLUTTER_API_URL'</span>);</pre>
      <p>The order is: the name on the command line, then <code>GITVAULT_ENV</code>, then the build mode. <code>flutter run</code>, <code>flutter test</code> and <code>--debug</code> are debug. <code>flutter build</code>, <code>--release</code> and <code>--profile</code> are release. Change the two defaults with <code>--debug-env</code> and <code>--release-env</code>.</p>
      <p>Everything passed to an app is compiled into it, so anyone who gets the app can read it. For that reason only variables that start with <code>FLUTTER_</code> are passed. Name the values that are safe to ship that way, and keep real server secrets out of client apps. <code>--prefix MY_</code> changes the prefix, and <code>--prefix ''</code> passes everything.</p>
      <p>Values travel in a private temporary file that is deleted when the command ends. They are never in an argument or an environment variable, and Flutter does not inherit your vault password or keys. The log shows only how many variables were loaded. The IDE's run button skips the wrapper, so start from a terminal.</p>

      <h3 id="other" class="sub">Other languages, scripts and tools</h3>
      <p>Use the CLI directly. Secrets are injected for that one process and never printed:</p>
      <pre class="code"><span class="g">$</span> gitvault run --environment production -- python app.py
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault run --environment development -- docker compose up
<span class="g">$</span> gitvault --json env export production <span class="c"># machine-readable, for tooling</span></pre>
      <p>AI tools can use secrets without seeing the values through the MCP server: <code>gitvault mcp</code>.</p>

      <h3 id="servers" class="sub">Servers and CI</h3>
      <p>Nothing interactive is needed. Set <code class="green">GITVAULT_TOKEN</code> (a read-only GitHub token for the vault repo) and <code class="green">GITVAULT_DEVICE_KEY</code> (from <code>gitvault device trust --name prod-server --export</code>). Both are removed from the process after use, and you can revoke a key any time with <code>gitvault device revoke</code>. <code>GITVAULT_PASSWORD</code> also works. A locked vault never prompts: it fails with <code>E_LOCKED</code>.</p>
      <p>To limit a developer machine to one project, run <code>gitvault trust add . --env development</code> inside it. The device then unlocks by itself only in that folder.</p>

      <div class="next">
        <a href={href('/security/')}><b>Security model →</b><span>How your secrets are encrypted and stored.</span></a>
        <a href={href('/changelog/')}><b>Changelog →</b><span>What changed in each release.</span></a>
      </div>
    </article>
    <aside class="toc"><h4>ON THIS PAGE</h4>{sections.map(([id, t]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{t}</a>)}</aside>
  </div>
</Base>
